{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"darkish-finable-chimpanzee","creation_channel":"cli","description":"The inbound UPDATE path never mints an identity for an inbound assignee: it hands the mint a\nbare STRING, and the mint's first line requires a dict. The mint returns immediately and\nsilently, because it is best-effort by contract. The inbound CREATE path is unaffected, which\nis exactly why this has gone unseen.\n\nFOUND BY: J11's live cell `test_the_inbound_assignee_mints_a_jira_family_identity`\n(tests/external/live_jira_dc/test_dc_mutations.py) on harness run 30767546938, job 91548842532,\nmain @ d72c4777d3 — the first run in which that cell's precondition held and it reached its real\nassertion. 48 passed / 1 failed, `[jira-live-canary] collected=49 executed=49 skipped=0`.\n\n    AssertionError: THE PASS MINTED NOTHING: jira/'admin' still resolves to no identity in the\n    store copy after an inbound pass that carried that assignee.\n\nMECHANISM — PROVEN, NOT INFERRED. Each link is cited, and the chain is reproduced harness-free\nin `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/test_inbound_update_assignee_mint_5200.py` (4 cells, green):\n\n  1. `inbound_fields._map_jira_to_local_fields:223-225` maps the Jira user object to TWO\n     canonical keys — the scalar `assignee` (a bare string from `_extract_jira_field_value`:\n     `name`, else `displayName`) and the fixed-shape `assignee_identity`\n     ({display, email, account_id}), whose `account_id` ALREADY carries Data Center's username\n     (`_identity_of:154-179`, the precedence 5f48 established).\n  2. `inbound_differ._diff_jira_vs_local:185-188` emits ONLY the scalar:\n     `changed[\"assignee\"] = jira_mapped.get(\"assignee\")`. `assignee_identity` is not in\n     `field_map` (`:170-176`), so it never reaches the mutation.\n  3. `apply_inbound_records._inbound_update_write_edit_event:333-340` states that these fields\n     ARE the differ's local-keyed shape, and `:369` passes `fields[\"assignee\"]` — that string —\n     into `_ensure_inbound_assignee_identity`.\n  4. `_ensure_inbound_assignee_identity:100-101` opens with\n     `if not isinstance(assignee, dict): return`. A string returns before any key is read.\n     Nothing is minted and nothing is logged: `:83-102` documents the function as\n     \"ADDITIVE + best-effort ... any mint failure is swallowed\".\n\n  The CREATE path at `:200-203` passes the RAW user object out of the snapshot, which IS a\n  dict, so an inbound create mints correctly. Every identity the J11 harness has ever produced\n  came from that path — including the one that made this cell's ORIGINAL precondition fail.\n\nWHY THIS IS NOT A SETUP ARTIFACT OF THE CELL. The suspicion was that the cell's unassign/sync\nstep leaves the pass with no assignee CHANGE to act on. It does not: cell 1 of the reproduction\nruns the real `_diff_jira_vs_local` with a local ticket that is unassigned and a Jira issue\nassigned to admin — precisely the state the cell arranges — and the differ DOES emit\n`changed[\"assignee\"]`. So the mint is reached; it declines the value it is given.\n\nWHY IT IS NOT A REGRESSION OF 5f48-244c-7063-42d0. That bug fixed the KEY SELECTION inside the\nmint (`for key in (\"accountId\", \"name\")`) so a DC user object resolves. This defect is upstream\nof that guard: on the update path the function returns at line 100 before any key is read, so\n5f48's fix is never reached. It is a case 5f48 never covered.\n\nSCOPE. The differ and the applier are deployment-neutral, so Cloud has the same gap on the\nupdate path; DC ships it, which is why it belongs to this epic (e369's standing precedent for\ndefects in paths DC ships). Its practical effect: a user first seen on an inbound EDIT (rather\nthan on the issue's inbound create) never gets a ghost identity, so a later outbound pass has\nnothing to key on — the exact failure mode 2f13's ghost-minting exists to prevent.\n\nPROPOSED FIX (NOT YET IMPLEMENTED — needs sign-off; no src/ change has been made).\nPlumb the identity through instead of loosening the guard:\n  * `inbound_differ._diff_jira_vs_local` — when it emits an `assignee` change, ALSO forward\n    `jira_mapped[\"assignee_identity\"]` (the mapper already computes it, at no extra extraction);\n  * `apply_inbound_records:369` — key the mint on that canonical identity's `account_id` /\n    `display` rather than on the scalar.\nDO NOT \"fix\" this by making `_ensure_inbound_assignee_identity` accept a bare string. On Data\nCenter the scalar happens to be `name` (== the external id), so it would look correct here; on\nCloud the scalar is the DISPLAY NAME, and minting keyed on it would register every Cloud user\nunder the wrong external id — a silent data defect strictly worse than the missing mint.\nThe reproduction's 4th cell pins the contract a correct fix must meet.\n\nACCEPTANCE CRITERIA\n  * An inbound pass that carries an assignee CHANGE (not only an inbound create) mints/reuses a\n    placeholder identity under the shared `jira` family, keyed on the deployment's external id\n    (accountId on Cloud, username on DC), and NOT under `jira-datacenter`.\n  * `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/test_inbound_update_assignee_mint_5200.py` is updated: cells 2\n    and 4 invert (the update path mints; the differ forwards the identity), and cell 3 stays\n    green so the create path is shown not to regress.\n  * A Cloud-shaped payload ({accountId, displayName} with displayName != accountId) mints keyed\n    on the accountId — the guard against the wrong fix above.\n  * The J11 live cell `test_the_inbound_assignee_mints_a_jira_family_identity` goes green on a\n    harness run WITHOUT any weakening of its assertions.","id":"8d68-a740-4f56-4b3a","parent_id":"e369-a449-4773-48fb","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"inbound UPDATE never mints an assignee identity: the mint gets a string"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1785709683304539001,"uuid":"64b0d430-c528-4238-a78a-89f87d6e5453"}